## DESCRIPTION
## Find the inverse of a matrix by the product of all elementary matrices used to row 
## reduce a matrix and then use the inverse to find the solution. 
## ENDDESCRIPTION


## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Matrices)
## DBsection(Inverses)
## Date(September 5, 2008)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## KEYWORDS('lines')

# updated 2023.11.02

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'MatrixReduce.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Point")->noreduce('(-x)-y', '(-x)+y');

do {
    $A = Matrix([
        [ random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), 0 ],
        [ 0,             random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3) ],
        [ random(-3, 3), 0,             random(-3, 3) ]
    ]);
} until grep { abs($A->det) == $_ } (1, 2, 4, 5);

do {
    ($x, $y, $z) = (random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3), random(-3, 3));
    $b = $A * Vector($x, $y, $z);
} until Vector($b) != Vector(0, 0, 0);

$line1 =
    Formula($A->element(1, 1) . 'x+'
        . $A->element(1, 2) . 'y+'
        . $A->element(1, 3)
        . 'z')->reduce;
$line2 =
    Formula($A->element(2, 1) . 'x+'
        . $A->element(2, 2) . 'y+'
        . $A->element(2, 3)
        . 'z')->reduce;
$line3 =
    Formula($A->element(3, 1) . 'x+'
        . $A->element(3, 2) . 'y+'
        . $A->element(3, 3)
        . 'z')->reduce;

$inv      = $A->inverse;
$solution = Point($x, $y, $z);

BEGIN_PGML
Consider the linear system 

[```  
\begin{aligned}
[$line1] & = [@ Matrix($b)->element(1) @] \\
[$line2] & = [@ Matrix($b)->element(2) @] \\
[$line3] & = [@ Matrix($b)->element(3) @] \\
\end{aligned}
```]

First, write this system as a augmented coefficient matrix.  Then multiply this matrix by a sequence of elementary matrices to transform the matrix to one in _reduced row echelon form_.  Call these matrices [` M_1,M_2, \ldots, M_k `].

a)  Find the product of all the matrices: [`M_k M_{k-1}\cdots M_2 M_1 =`] [_]*{$inv} 

b) Find the solution to your linear system (write your answer as a point): [_]{$solution}

END_PGML

Context('Fraction');
$AA = apply_fraction_to_matrix_entries($A);
$Ainv = $AA->inverse; 
$sol_vect = ColumnVector($x,$y,$z);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) There are two ways to do this.  1) Find all of the elementary matrices that reduce
the augmented matrix to one of reduced row echelon form.  In this case, multiply all
of the matrices in reverse order to get the result.  2) The product of these matrices
create the inverse matrix.  Either way that you get this, the result is

[``` [$Ainv] ```]

b) Now that we have the inverse, find the product of the inverse and the vector of the 
right hand side to get
[``` [$Ainv] [$b] = [$sol_vect] ```] 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
